#limitation(title: [The "Not Lifelong Onset" Criterion May Incorrectly Exclude Mild and Constitutional Cases])[
Diagnostic criteria across Fukuda, Canadian Consensus, and IOM require fatigue of *new or definite onset* (not lifelong) @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue. This onset criterion assumes a categorical boundary between a "constitutional" lifetime energy profile and an acquired illness. That boundary is difficult to defend operationally and risks excluding the mild, lifelong-energy-constrained patient for whom it was designed.

*Evidence.* Sunnquist et al.\ (2015) tested the operationalization of lifelong fatigue as an exclusion criterion in a large ME/CFS cohort @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue. The criterion proved hard to operationalize, was rarely met even by patients who described long-standing fatigue, and those with vs.\ without lifelong fatigue differed little in symptom profile or functional impact. The authors recommend removing lifelong fatigue as an exclusion.

*Implication.* A patient who has always run on a low energy reserve ("just more tired than expected") can still develop ME/CFS with a definite new PEM component @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue. PEM — not the pre-morbid energy baseline — is the discriminating feature. Requiring "not lifelong" onset may therefore delay or deny diagnosis for the mild/constitutional patient whose condition is real but whose baseline was never high.

*(Severity applicability: mild / lifetime-energy presentations. The moderate-to-severe transition is not addressed by this evidence.)*

*Consequence:* Clinicians should not dismiss ME/CFS merely because the patient reports having always been tired; the decisive feature is the new onset of post-exertional malaise, not the pre-illness energy baseline.
] <lim:lifelong-onset-criterion>
